@@ -1125,6 +1127,31 @@ When the `-remoteWrite.disableOnDiskQueue` command-line flag is set, `vmagent` m
 if it cannot keep up with the data ingestion rate. In this case [deduplication](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#deduplication)
 must be enabled on all the configured remote storage systems.
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+Service discovery sources (Kubernetes API, Consul, cloud provider APIs, etc.) sometimes misbehave because of misconfiguration
+on the discovery side — for example, a tag filter is accidentally removed, or a catalog query suddenly becomes too broad.
+When this happens, the number of scrape targets discovered for the affected job can spike dramatically, and `vmagent`
+would normally try to scrape all of them at once, which can spike memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every
+other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+As a safety net, `vmagent` can limit the number of active scrape targets a single job (`job_name`) is allowed to have
+at once, regardless of which service discovery mechanism produced the targets. This can be configured in two ways:
+
+* Globally, via the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This sets the default limit for all jobs.
+* Per job, via the `max_scrape_targets` option at [scrape_configs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+  section. It overrides `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` for the given job, including explicitly disabling the limit
+  for the job by setting it to `0`.
+
+By default, both options are set to `0`, meaning there is no limit, so existing setups aren't affected.
+
+When the number of targets discovered for a job exceeds the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to that
+number of targets for the job, instead of crashing, rejecting the whole job, or silently scraping everything. The excess
+targets are dropped and shown as dropped targets with reason `job target limit` at the `http://vmagent:8429/targets`
+and `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` pages — the same pages that already show targets dropped because of relabeling
+or [cluster sharding](#scraping-big-number-of-targets) — so it is easy to notice which job(s) are currently over the limit
+and roughly how many targets are being excluded because of it.
+
 ## Cardinality limiter
 
 By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of time series each scrape target can expose.
